[Event "Knight School"]
[Opening "French Defence: Advance Variation"]
[Variation "Advance Variation, Euwe Variation"]
[ECO "C02"]
[Character "positional,solid"]
[Risk "low"]
[EarlyMate "none"]
[Tricks "none"]
[Themes "pawn-chain,space,kingside-attack"]
[For "white"]
[Kind "main"]
[Opponent "3...c5 4.c3 Nc6 5.Nf3 Bd7"]
[Start "advance-qb6 4...Nc6 5.Nf3"]

5... Bd7 ( 5... cxd4 6. cxd4 { Take back with the pawn to keep the centre. } 6... Bb4+ ( 6... Nge7 7. Na3 { Heads for c2, where the knight guards d4 again without blocking the c-file. } 7... Nf5 8. Nc2 { Now d4 has three guards: the queen and both knights. Plan: Bd3 and castle. } ) ( 6... Qb6 7. Nc3 { Develops. The d4 pawn looks loose, but taking it loses a piece. } 7... Bd7 { The same position as 4...cxd4 5.cxd4 Nc6 6.Nf3 Bd7 7.Nc3 Qb6, where White plays a3. } ( 7... Nxd4 $2 8. Nxd4 { Black's queen cannot take back on d4, because White's queen on d1 would take it. White is a knight up. } ) ) 7. Bd2 { Block the check with the bishop; trading it off is fine for White. } 7... Bxd2+ 8. Qxd2 Nge7 9. Nc3 { Develops and guards e4 and d5. } 9... O-O { The same position as an Alapin Sicilian line (1.e4 c5 2.c3 e6 3.d4 cxd4 4.cxd4 Bb4+), where White plays Bd3 and castles, keeping d4 guarded by the queen and both knights. } ) ( 5... f6 6. Bd3 { Develops and aims at the king side. If Black takes on e5, White retakes in the centre and the bishop on d3 does the rest. } 6... fxe5 7. dxe5 ) 6. Be2 { Black has not yet put the queen on b6, so White has time for a quiet developing move and castling. } 6... Nge7 ( 6... cxd4 7. cxd4 Nge7 8. Nc3 { Another guard for d4, and the knight heads for b5 or a4 later. } 8... Nf5 9. O-O ) 7. O-O { Castles first; Black's attacks on d4 are not dangerous while the pieces behind it are guarded. } 7... Ng6 ( 7... Nf5 8. dxc5 { Black's knight left e7, so White can take on c5 safely. } 8... Bxc5 9. Bd3 ) 8. Be3 { Guards d4 with a bishop and keeps b2 safe. White now has three guards on d4: pawn, knight and bishop. } 8... Be7 9. Nbd2 { Brings the last piece out. From d2 the knight can go to b3 to add yet another guard on d4. } 9... O-O 10. Re1 { The rook backs up the e5 pawn, which Black's knights and f-pawn will try to hit. } 10... a5 11. a3 { Plan: keep d4 guarded, answer ...f6 with Bd3 or exf6, and think about h4-h5 to chase Black's knight from g6. Do not trade the pawn on d4 unless it helps White. } *
